# Fetched corpus content — ~49MB, re-fetchable from raw/manifest.json.
# NOTE the trailing /* rather than /: git does not descend into an ignored
# DIRECTORY, so a negation below it can never match. Ignoring the CONTENTS is
# what makes the exception reachable.
packages/corpus/data/*
packages/corpus/raw/*
# ...except the HARVEST artefacts. These are not re-fetchable cache: a row records what a source
# page said on a date, and the upstream API will not say it again.
#
# `asset-seeds.json` was the third one, and it went on 2026-09-20 with the asset catalogue it fed.
!packages/corpus/data/template-seeds.json
# ...and the GENRE-KIT PIN LEDGER, which is the one piece of the catalogue's evidence that outlived
# it. It is what Roblox's own details endpoint SAID about the fifty audio ids the genre kits pin —
# existence, asset type, licence, creator, duration — on a recorded date. The kits still reference
# those ids and `apps/worker/tests/genre-kit-pins.test.mjs` still checks every pin against it, and
# a guard whose evidence is not in the checkout is a guard that reports clean without looking.
#
# It lived at `data/library/kit-pins.json` until the catalogue was removed; the directory went with
# the catalogue and this file moved up one level rather than being deleted with it.
!packages/corpus/data/kit-pins.json
# ...and the CURATION LEDGER over that harvest, for the same reason one step further along. It
# holds a named verdict for all 3,017 rows — which rule excluded each, and which word fired it —
# so "131 survived" is a claim anybody can argue with row by row instead of a number to trust.
# It is not reproducible either: it was built by reading 653 repositories' file trees live, and
# a repository that is renamed, deleted or rewritten next year will not read the same way.
!packages/corpus/data/mechanic-library.json
# ...and the UI REFERENCE LIBRARY, which is the same kind of thing again: a record of what shipped
# Roblox interfaces actually look like, and the construction rules read off them. It is not
# re-derivable — the observations were made by looking at screenshots of games that will change,
# and the point of the library is that a design can be argued with reference by reference instead
# of being an agent's taste. Untracked, it would be one machine's scratch notes.
#
# A directory, so the negation has to re-include the DIRECTORY itself: `data/*` ignores the entry,
# and git never descends into an excluded directory. Same trap as the dataset cards further down.
!packages/corpus/data/ui-references/
# Authored, inspected reference metadata is required by the Worker runtime import and is not
# reproducible harvested media. Keep the manifest available in a fresh checkout.
!packages/corpus/data/genre-references.json
# ...and the WITNESS over chunks.jsonl, which is the other half of the manifest above. The manifest
# claims a url, a kind, a title and an exact chunk-id list for 25 documents in the local corpus, and
# the test that made those claims true read `chunks.jsonl` — 10 MB of gitignored build artefact. In
# a fresh checkout that threw ENOENT instead of failing an assertion, `pnpm -r test` bailed at
# @golem/corpus, and every package after it in the recursion never ran. This file is the 25
# documents, the corpus sha256 and the document count, so the claim is checkable without the corpus
# and is re-derived from the corpus wherever the corpus exists. See src/chunk-witness.mjs.
!packages/corpus/data/chunks-witness.json

# COMMITTED ON PURPOSE, unlike verified-modules.json beside it.
# verified-modules.json is regenerated by scripts/build-verified-modules.mjs, which RUNS the modules
# against their own checks locally and touches no network — so a clean clone rebuilds it for free.
# need-index.json is different in kind: packages/training/src/build-need-index.mjs generates the
# customer-phrasing paraphrases by CALLING THE DEPLOYED MODEL, which is a paid provider. This
# repository's standing rule is that CI never calls a paid provider, and a clean clone must be able
# to build the worker. An artifact whose generator costs money is an artifact that ships.
# It is 52KB. That is the whole cost of the decision.
!packages/corpus/data/need-index.json
# Built by scripts/build-ui-construction.mjs from ui-references/. It is COMMITTED because
# ui-construction-guide.ts imports it statically — esbuild embeds it in the Worker bundle, and a
# Worker has no filesystem to read the source directory from at runtime.
!packages/corpus/data/ui-construction.json
# Built by scripts/build-verified-modules.mjs. COMMITTED for the same reason: verified-modules.ts
# imports it statically and a Worker has no filesystem.
!packages/corpus/data/verified-modules.json
# THE ASSET CATALOGUE LIVED HERE AND WAS DELETED ON 2026-09-20.
#
# `packages/corpus/data/library/` held the harvests behind the curated asset library: 510,014 rows
# across Kenney, OpenGameArt, cgbookcase, game-icons, iconify and a Creator Store scrape, plus the
# index.json that published their counts. The owner removed the library outright — every upload it
# could make was an Image or a Decal, Roblox refuses to archive either, and the catalogue carried
# rows named after other companies' properties under one blanket licence claim.
#
# The directory and its six tracked files are gone. `kit-pins.json` was the seventh and is the only
# one that survived, one level up; see its note above.
#
# ...except the CHECKOUT LOCK. The comment above has always said the corpus is
# "re-fetchable from raw/manifest.json" — but `packages/corpus/raw/` (with the
# trailing slash this file's own note warns against, four lines up) ignored the
# directory, so the manifest went with it and a fresh clone had nothing to fetch
# FROM. It pins a url and a commit SHA per checkout: our metadata about third-party
# repositories, not their content, so tracking it redistributes nothing. It is what
# `pnpm --filter @golem/corpus bootstrap` reads.
!packages/corpus/raw/manifest.json
# ...and the HUGGING FACE lock, for the same reason and on the same terms. It pins each
# harvested dataset by url + commit SHA, and records the sources that were fetched, read and
# REFUSED with the grounds for refusing. The chunks it produces stay ignored (they are a build
# artefact, like chunks.jsonl); this is the metadata that makes the harvest reproducible and
# the refusals reviewable, so a source cannot quietly change under the product.
!packages/corpus/raw/manifest.hf.json
# ...except the source LEDGER. sources.json is 0.4MB of derived provenance and
# licence verdicts, and §H requires that metadata be preserved rather than
# regenerated on demand — a classification nobody can diff is one nobody reviews.
!packages/corpus/data/sources.json
!packages/corpus/data/registries.json
# The ContentRecord ledger: which sources are one idea, which diverged. Small, because the
# per-file hashes it derives from are regenerable and are not persisted.
!packages/corpus/data/content.json
# The registry handoff: 1,082 resolvable candidates. Tracked so `discover --includeRegistry`
# works without re-cloning 43MB of package indexes first.
!packages/corpus/data/registry-seeds.json
# THE UI ASSET INDEX over the GitHub corpus: 1,060 rights-cleared Luau files that build a Roblox
# interface, keyed by what they construct, each pinned to a revision and carrying its SPDX id and
# the sha256 of the licence document that granted it.
#
# Tracked because it is the LIBRARY, not a cache of one. The 25.9 MiB of source it points at stays
# out (rows.jsonl is ignored below for the same reason), so if this index is untracked there is
# nothing in a fresh clone to look anything up in, and `ui-asset-library.test.mjs` skips its
# arithmetic check while reporting green — a guard that passes by not finding its evidence.
# It is 1.1 MB, and it is re-derivable only by re-reading 1,022 repositories over the network.
!packages/corpus/data/ui-assets-github-v1.json
packages/evals/results/raw/

# THE HARVESTED ROBLOXQA ROWS: derived, third-party, and 4.8 MB. The EVIDENCE about them is not.
#
# `scripts/harvest-hf.mjs` pulls TorpedoSoftware/RobloxQA-v2.0 into gate.jsonl (1.9 MB) and
# headroom.jsonl (2.9 MB). Both are re-fetchable from the pinned `revision` in dataset-card.json,
# and neither is ours to redistribute simply because it is convenient.
#
# The reason this rule has to exist rather than being assumed: `qa-overlap.test.mjs` already
# explains its own fallback path with the words "the derived .jsonl rows are gitignored" — and
# until now no rule said so, so a `git add packages/evals/data/` would have committed 4.8 MB of
# someone else's dataset and the test's stated reasoning would have been false. The small files
# beside them — question-keys.json, excluded-gate-rows.json, train-overlap.json, dataset-card.json
# — are the committed evidence those tests actually read, and they stay.
packages/evals/data/robloxqa/*.jsonl

# Playwright
test-results/
playwright-report/

# Throwaway measurement scaffolding
measure-tmp.mjs

# Agent worktrees — isolated checkouts, never part of the committed tree
.claude/worktrees/

# Training: local venv, model weights, datasets and adapters. Re-buildable from
# src/build-dataset.mjs + the pinned corpus manifest; none of it belongs in git.
packages/training/.venv/
packages/training/adapters/
packages/training/mlxdata/

# THE TRAINING ARTEFACTS: large and derived are ignored, small and CITED are not.
#
# runs/ and data/ were ignored wholesale alongside adapters/, and adapters/ is 210 MB of
# safetensors so that rule is right for it. runs/ is 36 KB of plain-text logs, and four backlog
# rows cite those logs as their only evidence — so ignoring them meant four rows nobody but the
# machine that ran the training could ever verify. That is collateral from a rule aimed at
# something else.
#
# data/ stays ignored because train/val/test.jsonl are DERIVED — build-dataset.mjs regenerates
# them — but dataset-card.json is 8 KB, is cited by a row, and is the provenance record for a
# dataset that is not itself tracked. It is re-included by name.
packages/training/data/*
!packages/training/data/dataset-card.json
# Same reason, one level down: a built dataset lives in its own directory and carries its own
# card. The card is the only record of what the data is, what verified it and what it does NOT
# establish; leaving it inside the ignored directory means the provenance never reaches the
# repository while the claims built on it do.
#
# Three lines rather than one, because git will not re-include a file inside an EXCLUDED
# DIRECTORY — it never descends into it, so `!.../*/dataset-card.json` on its own silently does
# nothing. So: let the subdirectories be descended, exclude their contents, then re-include the
# cards. Verified both ways with `git check-ignore -v`: a card is tracked, a train.jsonl is not.
!packages/training/data/*/
packages/training/data/*/*
!packages/training/data/*/dataset-card.json
# ...and THE ONE PREREGISTERED DATASET, with the preregistration beside it. Everything above turns
# on "train/val/test.jsonl are DERIVED — build-dataset.mjs regenerates them". These 28 rows are the
# exception: `apple-max-contract-v4-preregistration-2026-09-18.json` FREEZES them, by sha256, per
# file and per holdout row, before any training ran. A freeze is only worth the paper it is on if
# somebody other than the machine that made it can check it, and
# `contract-generalization-curriculum.test.mjs` does exactly that — it re-hashes every named file
# and every holdout row against the record.
#
# Untracked, that test threw ENOENT in every clone, which took `pnpm -r test` down at
# @golem/training and stopped @golem/worker and @golem/site from running at all. 71 KB in total,
# first-party-authored-synthetic, customerData false, and its own card says so.
!packages/training/data/apple-max-contract-v4-preregistration-2026-09-18.json
!packages/training/data/game-logic-seeds-v4-contract/*.jsonl
# Same rule, same reason, for the Hugging Face harvest: `scripts/harvest-hf.mjs` writes its
# REFUSALS to data/hf/REJECTED.json — seven rejected datasets with the measurement that killed each
# one, and the served-or-not verdict on five Roblox models. That file is the entire evidence for
# every "we did not use this" decision, and it was landing inside an ignored directory, so none of
# it would ever have reached the repository. A failure file nobody can read is the same shape as no
# failure file. The directory is re-included; any ADMITTED rows it may hold in future are large and
# derived, and stay out.
!packages/training/data/hf/
packages/training/data/hf/*.jsonl
# ...and the GITHUB ACQUISITION LEDGER, on exactly the terms above. `acquire-github-luau.mjs`
# writes two files per corpus directory. `rows.jsonl` is third-party Luau source and stays out:
# it is not ours to redistribute, and `repos.jsonl` pins the commit every row came from, so it is
# re-fetchable by anyone holding the ledger.
#
# `repos.jsonl` is the ledger, and it is the opposite kind of file. Per repository it records the
# pinned commit, the path of the LICENSE that was read, the sha256 of that licence TEXT, and
# whether the text corroborated the SPDX id GitHub detected — plus every repository that was
# REFUSED and the grounds. It is the entire evidence for the rights tier the corpus claims. A
# licence verdict that only exists on the machine that made it is a verdict nobody can review.
!packages/training/data/*/repos.jsonl
packages/training/mlxdata-text/
packages/training/mlxdata/

# nonstop (machine-specific)
.claude/settings.local.json
.nonstop/

# unlazy Stop hook session state — machine-specific, and tracking it makes every tree dirty
.unlazy-hook-state.json
.unlazy/

# Pixel frames are written on every check-pixels run and a full capture is ~21MB. The BASELINE is
# committed, because a regression is measured against it, and §16.10 wants the final pass's capture
# committed as evidence. Interim captures are local: they exist to be looked at, not to be carried
# in history. They are ignored rather than deleted — §18 forbids discarding an untracked path, and
# a frame somebody is mid-way through reviewing is exactly the kind of thing that rule protects.
docs/evidence/pixels/*
!docs/evidence/pixels/baseline/
!docs/evidence/pixels/final/

# graphify's generated map. `graphify update .` rewrites graph.json (6MB), graph.html and an AST
# cache on every run, and nothing in this repository reads any of it — checked with grep across
# scripts/, apps/*/src, tests/ and packages/*/src before ignoring it. Tracking it meant a ~12MB
# diff per rebuild for an artifact that regenerates from source in about a minute.
#
# GRAPH_REPORT.md stays tracked: it is small, it is the part a person reads, and its "built from
# commit" line is what tells you the rest is stale.
graphify-out/*
!graphify-out/GRAPH_REPORT.md
handoff/

# The fix queue: 844 work items with file paths, written by the auditing agents from the code as
# it stood at one moment. It is scaffolding for a run, not a record — the record is the checklist
# in docs/backlog and the commits that close its lines. Tracking it would put a stale copy of the
# codebase's own state under version control, and the next audit rewrites all of it.
.superpowers/fixq/

# The template curator's tree cache. `scripts/curate-templates.mjs` reads 653 repository file
# trees over the network; the cache exists so the CURATION RULES can be re-run in a second without
# re-reading GitHub. It holds only what GitHub returned, never a verdict, and `--refresh` discards
# it — a cached answer must never be the reason a repository stays in the library.
.cache/
.screens/

# Python bytecode. Fourteen .pyc files were tracked, which is how `git status` came to show
# `M scripts/__pycache__/secret-scan.cpython-39.pyc` every time anyone ran the secret scanner —
# a tracked file that rewrites itself on use makes every working tree permanently dirty, and a
# permanently dirty tree is how an unstaged fix gets mistaken for a committed one. They are also
# compiled copies of source the scanner already reads, so they doubled the credential surface
# for nothing. Deleting them costs a recompile.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Autonomy harness runtime state (docs/autonomy/README.md). Durable knowledge lives in docs/autonomy/.
.autonomy/

# scratch artefacts (public-repo scan, 2026-09-23)
.tmp-*
.playwright-mcp/
.workbuddy-ai/
orgsweep.tsv
claude-autonomy-research-pack/
